\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Constructive regularity and the reflected positive form}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Axioms A-C in \citet{pp. 273-275}{frohlich1974verification}
hold for the state #{\omega} and functional #{J} of \ref{fdip-00I6}.
In particular, let
#{\theta(x,t)=(x,-t)} act on distributions by pullback and let
#{\mathcal E_+} be the complex linear span of the bounded observables
#{\ue^{\ui \Phi(f)}} with real Schwartz #{f} supported at nonnegative
times. The reflected sesquilinear form
##{B(F,G)=\omega\bigl(\overline{F\circ\theta}\,G\bigr)}
is positive on #{\mathcal E_+}. This is the continuum version of the
\vocabk{reflection-positive pairing}{fdip-007M}.}

\p{The sharp-time fields form linear maps of spatial Schwartz
tests into #{L^2(\nu)}. Their two-point form is continuous in both
time arguments, compatible with ordinary time smearing, and obeys
##{|\omega(\Phi_{t_1}(h_1)\Phi_{t_2}(h_2))|
      \le C n_H(h_1)n_H(h_2).}
For the time cutoffs #{g_T} specified in the proof, there are
#{K,c>0} such that
##{J(\pm\ui h\otimes g_T)\le K\ue^{cT}
 \qquad(T\ge0,\ h\text{ real Schwartz},\ n_H(h)\le1).}
The constants depend on the fixed model and are independent of
#{h,T} in this normalized class.}

\proof{
\p{For A1-A4, normalization, positivity and involution compatibility
hold because integration is a positive normalized state on the
commutative observable algebra. Thus #{J(0)=1}, #{J} is of positive
type and #{J(f)=\overline{J(-f)}}. Schwartz continuity follows from
\ref{fdip-00I6} and
\citet{Theorem 4.2(I)}{frohlich1974schwingerI}. For A5-A6, full
Euclidean invariance, including time translations and time reflection,
follows from Theorems 4.1(II), 4.2(I) of that source and the
directed-domain identification in \ref{fdip-00I6}. Invariance is
asserted of the limit state, not of a bounded square.}

\p{For B, first consider the canonical Dirichlet Gaussian on
#{(-a,a)^2}. Choose an orthonormal eigenbasis of the spatial
Dirichlet massive operator; its square-root frequencies are positive
numbers #{\omega}. In each mode the time Dirichlet Green function
obeys, for #{0<s,t<a},
##{G_\omega(-t,s)=
 \frac{\sinh(\omega(a-t))\sinh(\omega(a-s))}
      {\omega\sinh(2a\omega)}.}
This is a positive rank-one kernel on positive times. Summing modes
makes the reflected covariance form positive. For any finite list
of positive-time tests, use the standard basis of its complex
coefficient space. The Gaussian characteristic matrix factors into
positive diagonal factors #{\ue^{-C(f_i,f_i)/2}} and the entrywise
exponential of the positive matrix #{C(\theta f_i,f_j)}.
The exponential is positive by its power series and the Schur product
theorem. This proves Gaussian reflection positivity.}

\p{The whole-space Wick interaction splits as
#{V_\Lambda=V_{\Lambda,+}+V_{\Lambda,+}\circ\theta}.
The time-zero line has area zero and the Wick reference is reflection
invariant. Its positive-half weight #{W_+=\ue^{-V_{\Lambda,+}}}
is positive-time measurable and belongs to Gaussian #{L^2}.
The Wick conversion
\citet{(II.88), Theorem II.38(ii)-(iv),
pp. 172-173}{guerra1975euclideanI} rewrites the whole-space Wick
quartic in Dirichlet Wick powers. The additional quadratic and
constant coefficients are multiples of the boundary correction and
its square. Equation (II.88b) bounds that correction near the boundary
by a constant times #{|\log u(x)|}.}

\p{On the positive half-square the leading coefficient is the
positive constant #{\lambda /24} times its indicator. The ratios of
the lower coefficients to it are bounded by powers of
#{1+|\log u(x)|}. The weighted powers required in (II.89) are
therefore integrable by the square's log-normality from
\ref{fdip-00I4}; the coefficient #{L^{1+\delta}} conditions hold as
well. Restriction to the half-square preserves these estimates.
Theorem II.38(ii)-(iv) consequently gives all finite Gaussian
#{L^p} bounds for #{W_+} and convergence of local approximations.
Approximations supported at positive times retain positive-time
measurability; their #{L^2} limit does too. This justifies the
reflection factorization for exactly the canonical law of
\citet{(3.80)-(3.81)}{frohlich1974schwingerI}, with the same
argument as \citet{(3.19)}{frohlich1974verification}.}

\p{For a bounded positive-time cylinder #{F}, the reflected
interacting expectation is the Gaussian pairing of #{W_+F} with
itself, divided by #{Z_\Lambda>0}. Gaussian positivity extends
from bounded cylinders to the positive-time #{L^2} domain by
truncation and its Cauchy-Schwarz bound, exactly as in
[positive-time domain](fdip-007M). Thus the interacting form is positive. The
locally uniform characteristic convergence of \ref{fdip-00I6}
passes every finite such inequality to the volume limit. Tests whose
support touches time zero follow by smooth positive-time
approximation and Schwartz continuity.}

\p{For C1, \ref{fdip-00I6} gives entire finite-dimensional
functionals, continuous mixed derivatives and the jointly
Schwartz-continuous two-point distribution. The distinct sharp-time
input is \citet{Proposition 3.15(a), pp. 294-295}{frohlich1974schwingerI}:
uniformly in region and times,
##{|S_2^\Lambda(h_1\otimes\delta_{t_1},h_2\otimes\delta_{t_2})|
 \le C n_H(h_1)n_H(h_2).}
Theorem 4.4 and Corollary 4.5 of that source, pp. 298-300,
construct these sharp-time fields in the same limit state. Lemma 4.6
and Theorem 4.7, pp. 300-302, give continuity of their characteristic
functions and moments in all time arguments, and compatibility with
ordinary smearing. Their spatial norm is
#{\|h\|_1+\|h\|_\infty}, finite on Schwartz tests. It is separate
from #{G_H}, which is not applied to a delta-time test. These facts
give the required bounded measurable, indeed continuous, sharp-time
two-point form near equal times.}

\p{For C2, choose smooth even #{g} with #{0\le g\le1}, supported
in #{[-1,1]} and equal to one on #{[-1/2,1/2]}. For #{T\ge0}, put
#{g_T(t)=1} when #{|t|\le T} and
#{g_T(t)=g(|t|-T)} otherwise, as on p. 275 of
\citek{frohlich1974verification}. Positivity of the moment
coefficients and the absolute-test comparison bound the Laplace
transform for #{h\otimes g_T} by that for
#{|h|\otimes\mathbf1_{[-T-1,T+1]}}. Proposition 3.15(a) of
\citek{frohlich1974schwingerI} gives
##{J_\Lambda(\pm\ui h\otimes g_T)
 \le\exp\bigl[2(T+1)(K_4n_H(h)^2+K_5)\bigr].}
Enlarge #{K_4,K_5} to be nonnegative. On #{n_H(h)\le1}, take
#{c=2(K_4+K_5)} and #{K=\ue^c}.}

\p{The indicator-time Laplace transform is directly the object
bounded in Proposition 3.15(a). No smooth approximation of that
indicator in the time supremum norm is needed. Positivity of the
moment distributions gives comparison with the smooth #{g_T}
bounded by it; the nonsmooth spatial absolute value uses the
weighted-#{L^2} extension of the source estimates. Entire convergence
at the fixed smooth test #{h\otimes g_T} passes the bound to #{J}.
If #{n_1(h)\le1} is used instead, take
#{c=2(2K_4+K_5)} and enlarge #{K} accordingly. The spatial norm
is Schwartz-continuous and the required #{K_h \ue^{cT}} bound holds,
even with uniform #{K}. This constructive estimate is distinct from
finite-cutoff coercivity.}
}
